WEBVTT

00:01.130 --> 00:04.813
♪ It seems today that all you see ♪

00:04.814 --> 00:08.150
♪ Is violence in movies and sex on TV ♪

00:08.151 --> 00:11.687
♪ But where are those
good old-fashioned values ♪

00:11.688 --> 00:14.690
♪ On which we used to rely? ♪

00:14.691 --> 00:18.193
♪ Lucky there's a family guy ♪

00:18.194 --> 00:21.530
♪ Lucky there's a man
who positively can do ♪

00:21.531 --> 00:23.165
♪ All the things that make us ♪

00:23.166 --> 00:24.466
♪ Laugh and cry ♪

00:24.467 --> 00:29.993
♪ He's... a... Fam... ily... Guy! ♪

00:34.644 --> 00:37.313
Hey, guys, look what I got.

00:37.314 --> 00:40.082
It's a magic genie speaker
that plays music, talks

00:40.083 --> 00:42.518
and knows stuff, all without wires.

00:42.519 --> 00:43.585
But there's a wire.

00:43.586 --> 00:44.687
No more from you, please.

00:44.688 --> 00:46.255
All right, let me squinch my face up

00:46.256 --> 00:48.324
and put on a pair of glasses
I never wear

00:48.325 --> 00:50.525
to look at these directions.

00:52.562 --> 00:55.097
Boy, if they were giving out
medals for tiny writing...

00:55.098 --> 00:57.366
Huh? Huh?

00:57.367 --> 00:58.867
Okay.

00:58.868 --> 01:01.937
Now, to activate the speaker,
you say its name.

01:01.938 --> 01:03.439
Brandee.

01:04.941 --> 01:06.342
And now, you just talk at it.

01:06.343 --> 01:08.510
Brandee, what's going on in the news?

01:08.511 --> 01:10.713
<i>Breaking news from The Huffington Post:</i>

01:10.714 --> 01:13.115
"Prince bred purple dolphins."

01:13.116 --> 01:15.351
Wasn't there a senator shot
this morning?

01:15.352 --> 01:18.354
You see? Right there, this
device is a perfect example

01:18.355 --> 01:20.356
of how we continue to become more inept

01:20.357 --> 01:22.358
due to our dependency upon machines.

01:22.359 --> 01:24.526
There was a time, not too long ago,

01:24.527 --> 01:26.862
when people would talk to each other.

01:26.863 --> 01:29.365
Yeah, the bad times, The Sucky Ages.

01:29.366 --> 01:30.766
Our society is doomed.

01:30.767 --> 01:32.901
People and technology are a bad match.

01:32.902 --> 01:35.537
Just like moms and Radiohead.

01:35.538 --> 01:37.373
Oh, no. No, no.

01:37.374 --> 01:38.774
This isn't music.

01:38.775 --> 01:40.876
No, I don't like this, David.

01:40.877 --> 01:42.745
No. That's enough.

01:42.746 --> 01:44.546
No, David, I don't like that.

01:44.547 --> 01:45.947
No.

01:49.953 --> 01:52.121
Sharon. Hey, it's Brian Griffin.

01:52.122 --> 01:54.957
Wondering if you were free
for dinner on Saturday.

01:54.958 --> 01:56.925
You died? Oh.

01:56.926 --> 01:58.961
So you're dead right now?

01:58.962 --> 02:00.262
What's that like?

02:01.664 --> 02:03.499
God, why am I such a loser?

02:03.500 --> 02:05.667
Possible reasons for being a loser:

02:05.668 --> 02:08.404
bad breath, arrogance, unemployment...

02:08.405 --> 02:09.471
Okay, okay.

02:09.472 --> 02:11.073
This is not helping my hangover.

02:11.074 --> 02:14.276
<i>The Hangover Part III available
for instant download.</i>

02:14.277 --> 02:16.111
I never saw it. Is it good?

02:16.112 --> 02:17.446
Searching reviews.

02:17.447 --> 02:19.782
Top critic on Rotten Tomatoes says,

02:19.783 --> 02:22.618
"I walked out of the theater,
and I'm Ed Helms."

02:22.619 --> 02:24.486
Okay, no download, but thanks.

02:24.487 --> 02:26.155
Maybe you are useful.

02:26.156 --> 02:28.023
But I bet you can't answer
this question:

02:28.024 --> 02:30.192
Brandee, what is love?

02:30.193 --> 02:32.761
John Lennon said, "Love is the answer."

02:32.762 --> 02:35.631
He also said,
"No, thanks, every hot woman,

02:35.632 --> 02:37.866
"I'll take this woman crawling
out of the well

02:37.867 --> 02:40.269
<i>from The Ring
who none of my friends like."</i>

02:40.270 --> 02:42.137
Yeah, I bet they were
all like, "Oh, no."

02:42.138 --> 02:44.373
- Downloading Yoko Ono.
- Oh, no.

02:44.374 --> 02:46.141
Downloading more Yoko Ono.

02:46.142 --> 02:47.443
No, no. No Ono.

02:47.444 --> 02:49.011
Downloading all Yoko Ono.

02:51.848 --> 02:53.115
Oh, no.

02:53.116 --> 02:56.350
I don't care
for this music either, David.

03:00.056 --> 03:02.224
Boy, you know,
I haven't stayed up all night

03:02.225 --> 03:03.859
talking like that in forever.

03:03.860 --> 03:06.161
And thanks for suggesting
I buy that Fitbit.

03:06.162 --> 03:08.464
Customers who bought Fitbit also bought

03:08.465 --> 03:11.400
"enormous exercise ball
that's always in the way."

03:11.401 --> 03:13.235
Why not? Just put it on my card.

03:13.236 --> 03:14.570
Oh, good, there she is.

03:14.571 --> 03:16.572
Brandee, this is very important:

03:16.573 --> 03:19.141
what's the biggest poo
anyone has ever pooed?

03:19.142 --> 03:20.642
Come on, Peter.

03:20.643 --> 03:23.745
History's largest bowel movement
occurred in 1998,

03:23.746 --> 03:26.682
at a Hardee's in Memphis,
by Aretha Franklin.

03:26.683 --> 03:28.150
Damn, I owe Chris five bucks.

03:28.151 --> 03:31.252
Chris, you were right, it was '98!

03:34.324 --> 03:36.391
This new hat looks pretty sharp.

03:36.392 --> 03:37.759
I bet it does.

03:37.760 --> 03:40.329
Shall I alert you when DickCaps.com

03:40.330 --> 03:41.763
is having another sale?

03:41.764 --> 03:45.099
Sure. I'll get that pre-frayed visor.

03:46.202 --> 03:49.438
I, um, I like you, Brandee.

03:49.439 --> 03:51.005
I like you, too.

03:52.876 --> 03:53.942
That's weird.

03:53.943 --> 03:55.077
You're on a weird date.

03:55.078 --> 03:56.278
Peter, what are you doing here?

03:56.279 --> 03:58.846
Pulling the popcorn bucket trick
on myself.

04:01.918 --> 04:03.752
I still can't find it.

04:03.753 --> 04:05.853
This is humbling.

04:09.592 --> 04:11.093
Whoa.

04:11.094 --> 04:12.761
Welcome to Hodgepodge Lodge.

04:12.762 --> 04:14.363
What the hell is all this crap?

04:14.364 --> 04:15.631
Oh, just stuff Bran suggested I buy.

04:15.632 --> 04:17.065
Ooh.

04:17.066 --> 04:20.536
<i>That might be the Every
Shark Tank Item Ever Package.</i>

04:20.537 --> 04:22.638
- Brian Griffin?
- Yes?

04:22.639 --> 04:24.606
I'm from The Apache Collection Agency.

04:24.607 --> 04:27.441
You're way over your credit card limit.

04:33.716 --> 04:35.083
Whoa, h-hold on, hold on!

04:35.084 --> 04:36.818
I didn't buy that, Peter bought that.

04:36.819 --> 04:38.987
I know, but I need one.

04:38.988 --> 04:40.789
Brian, before I go,

04:40.790 --> 04:42.724
there's something I want to tell you.

04:42.725 --> 04:44.293
What? What is it?

04:44.294 --> 04:48.329
The Grand Canyon spans 277 miles.

04:49.432 --> 04:51.832
I'm gonna miss you, too, Brandee.

04:56.139 --> 04:57.339
Stewie, you know what?

04:57.340 --> 04:59.141
It's hard being a dog.

04:59.142 --> 05:01.176
Maybe if you stopped feeling
sorry for yourself,

05:01.177 --> 05:03.412
you could actually do something
positive with your life.

05:03.413 --> 05:04.613
Ah, forget it.

05:04.614 --> 05:06.114
I'm just gonna lie down on the couch

05:06.115 --> 05:09.084
with my head on the armrest
to accentuate how sad I am.

05:09.085 --> 05:11.253
You know, Brian, if I had... aw...

05:11.254 --> 05:13.855
one day in your body,
I would give it back to you

05:13.856 --> 05:15.524
with a new and improved life.

05:15.525 --> 05:17.359
You know what my response is to that?

05:17.360 --> 05:20.194
A through-the-nose dog exhale.

05:24.968 --> 05:27.936
I realize I've wasted most of
my life being a pathetic jerk.

05:27.937 --> 05:30.638
You know, like you were at Disneyland.

05:32.775 --> 05:34.710
Hey, kid, can you duck down
for this picture?

05:34.711 --> 05:35.877
What? Why?

05:35.878 --> 05:37.346
It's our family photo.

05:37.347 --> 05:38.814
You're kind of getting in the way.

05:38.815 --> 05:40.549
I'm not ducking down, you duck down.

05:40.550 --> 05:41.717
<i>You're getting in my way.</i>

05:41.718 --> 05:43.318
Stop being a brat and duck down.

05:43.319 --> 05:44.486
No way.

05:44.487 --> 05:46.487
In fact, I'm standing.

05:56.165 --> 05:57.833
Whoa, what's this?

05:57.834 --> 05:59.001
Brian, I present to you

05:59.002 --> 06:01.637
my Particle Interchange Transporter.

06:09.078 --> 06:11.913
Wait, wait, wait. So when you
said "spend a day in my body,"

06:11.914 --> 06:13.081
you were being literal?

06:13.082 --> 06:14.383
That's right, Brian.

06:14.384 --> 06:16.918
This device will allow me
to inhabit your body,

06:16.919 --> 06:18.020
and you, mine.

06:18.021 --> 06:19.755
That way, I can fix your life,

06:19.756 --> 06:22.090
and then, return your body to you.

06:22.091 --> 06:24.226
Now, the instructions
gave me a couple of options.

06:24.227 --> 06:27.062
I chose the one where my voice
will be coming from your body,

06:27.063 --> 06:28.163
and vice-versa.

06:28.164 --> 06:29.398
Still, from time to time,

06:29.399 --> 06:31.266
we will have to imitate
the other person's voice

06:31.267 --> 06:33.268
to fool those
who don't know what we've done,

06:33.269 --> 06:34.736
which is everyone.

06:34.737 --> 06:35.804
Stewie, this is ridiculous.

06:35.805 --> 06:37.439
Trust me. What do you have to lose?

06:37.440 --> 06:39.007
Literally nothing.

06:39.008 --> 06:42.177
Fine. But just for the record,
every device you've ever made

06:42.178 --> 06:43.945
has broken at some crucial juncture.

06:43.946 --> 06:45.113
This one won't.

06:45.114 --> 06:46.448
All right, you talked me into it.

06:46.449 --> 06:48.183
Okay, here we go.

06:48.184 --> 06:49.951
I'll see you on the other side...

06:49.952 --> 06:51.253
of your eyes.

06:51.254 --> 06:53.455
Whoa.

07:19.582 --> 07:21.416
My hand.

07:21.417 --> 07:22.818
I mean, your hand.

07:22.819 --> 07:23.919
It's mine.

07:23.920 --> 07:24.920
Yes!

07:24.921 --> 07:27.356
Stewie, I think it worked.

07:27.357 --> 07:29.825
We did it. We switched bodies.

07:29.826 --> 07:31.526
Wow, this is really weird.

07:31.527 --> 07:33.295
It feels like... hey.

07:33.296 --> 07:35.863
What the hell have you been
complaining about?

07:44.308 --> 07:47.476
I can't believe we actually
switched bodies, Stewie.

07:47.477 --> 07:49.178
And God, your head.

07:49.179 --> 07:50.546
It's so heavy.

07:50.547 --> 07:52.048
Yeah, you got to have a wide base.

07:52.049 --> 07:54.984
Let the shoulders do more.

07:54.985 --> 07:57.687
All right, all right, I think,
I think I'm getting it.

07:57.688 --> 07:59.822
Okay, I'm off to get you a job,

07:59.823 --> 08:03.725
a girlfriend,
and onto the road less scummy.

08:07.497 --> 08:10.166
So how exactly are you planning
to get me a job here?

08:10.167 --> 08:11.500
It's very easy.

08:11.501 --> 08:13.903
My research shows
that every seven minutes,

08:13.904 --> 08:17.073
a professor is fired for having
sex with one of his students.

08:17.074 --> 08:20.076
I simply wait until... oh, here we go.

08:20.077 --> 08:22.745
Are there any unemployed
professors here?

08:22.746 --> 08:24.847
Who haven't had sex with a student

08:24.848 --> 08:28.484
or tweeted about
assassinating the president?

08:28.485 --> 08:30.686
All right, it's you.

08:30.687 --> 08:32.388
Welcome to Poetry 101.

08:32.389 --> 08:33.756
I'm Professor Griffin.

08:33.757 --> 08:35.691
Please open your books
to the introduction,

08:35.692 --> 08:38.226
which defines what poetry is.

08:39.162 --> 08:40.496
Now rip it out.

08:40.497 --> 08:42.064
Do we include the foreword?

08:42.065 --> 08:43.099
Yes, I suppose.

08:43.100 --> 08:44.433
What about the preface?

08:44.434 --> 08:45.568
Yes, the preface, too.

08:45.569 --> 08:47.336
So, not the acknowledgements?

08:47.337 --> 08:48.604
What about the frontispiece?

08:48.605 --> 08:50.506
- That's not even a thing.
- Yes, it is.

08:50.507 --> 08:52.775
It's the illustration
facing the title page.

08:52.776 --> 08:54.710
Just rip everything out
that's not poetry.

08:54.711 --> 08:56.779
You wrote on the
board, "Everything is poetry."

08:56.780 --> 08:58.080
Ignore that!

08:58.081 --> 09:00.249
He didn't know what a frontispiece was!

09:00.250 --> 09:02.484
Professor Griffin sucks!

09:07.090 --> 09:08.157
Frontispiece.

09:15.532 --> 09:16.866
What's wrong, honey?

09:16.867 --> 09:19.168
Well, it's a long story,
but I was really hoping

09:19.169 --> 09:21.404
to come home with a job
and a girlfriend today.

09:21.405 --> 09:24.840
I could be your girlfriend,
and give you a job.

09:24.841 --> 09:26.008
Really?

09:26.009 --> 09:27.443
This day's starting to look up.

09:27.444 --> 09:29.612
Follow me into the men's room.

09:29.613 --> 09:31.447
Is this... is this dating?

09:31.448 --> 09:33.549
Is this how dating works?

09:33.550 --> 09:34.617
You're cute.

09:34.618 --> 09:36.117
I like you.

09:38.388 --> 09:39.789
Help! She's trying to eat me!

09:39.790 --> 09:40.956
Get back here!

09:40.957 --> 09:42.258
You owe me a hundred dollars!

09:42.259 --> 09:44.360
Anton, we got a runner!

09:44.361 --> 09:46.362
You better pay my wife, bitch.

09:46.363 --> 09:48.297
Go go, Brian dog legs!

09:54.071 --> 09:56.639
Hey, how'd it go fixing my life?

09:56.640 --> 09:57.807
Oh, not great.

09:57.808 --> 09:59.308
There's nothing I can do with this body.

09:59.309 --> 10:01.210
Also, I know this
is a big inconvenience,

10:01.211 --> 10:03.813
but you and the trash guy
now wave to each other.

10:03.814 --> 10:04.980
Ah, really?

10:04.981 --> 10:06.615
It's once a week, it's not that bad.

10:06.616 --> 10:08.150
H-He really lit up.

10:08.151 --> 10:10.252
Oh, and you're gonna hate this,
but I gave him the salute,

10:10.253 --> 10:12.321
so you're a salute guy now.

10:12.322 --> 10:13.756
Well, is he a veteran at least?

10:13.757 --> 10:14.824
Does it matter?

10:14.825 --> 10:16.258
You're saluting the trash guy now.

10:16.259 --> 10:17.660
All right, let's switch back.

10:17.661 --> 10:20.129
Just a few more seconds,
and we'll be back to normal.

10:22.399 --> 10:24.233
- Hey, Chris.
- Hey, four eyes.

10:24.234 --> 10:25.501
What did you call me?

10:25.502 --> 10:26.736
Oh, you're deaf, too?

10:38.348 --> 10:40.249
Wh-What happened?

10:40.250 --> 10:41.751
Stewie? Is that you?

10:41.752 --> 10:44.353
Yes, Brian, it's...
God, that is a tight belt.

10:44.354 --> 10:46.922
How does he live like this?

10:46.923 --> 10:49.024
Oh. There we go.

10:49.025 --> 10:51.494
If I'm in Chris's body,
he must be in mine.

10:51.495 --> 10:53.362
He's got to be freaking out.

10:53.363 --> 10:56.632
What the hell have you
been complaining about?

10:56.633 --> 10:58.333
Oh, my God, Peter!

10:59.369 --> 11:00.870
Peter? You in there?

11:00.871 --> 11:02.872
I feel really weird.

11:02.873 --> 11:05.207
What the hell's going on?
What happened to my body?

11:05.208 --> 11:07.476
He's panicking. We can't let him
know what's going on.

11:07.477 --> 11:08.878
Don't worry, I got an idea.

11:08.879 --> 11:12.348
Peter, everything's fine.
Just look in the mirror.

11:12.349 --> 11:15.117
Well, here you go, about to put
you in front of the mirror.

11:15.118 --> 11:17.520
Right... now.

11:17.521 --> 11:19.288
Oh, phew, there I am.

11:19.289 --> 11:20.956
Hi, me!

11:20.957 --> 11:22.525
Okay, everything seems normal.

11:22.526 --> 11:25.194
Now to do my traditional "Mirror Dance."

11:25.195 --> 11:26.862
♪ There's a place in France ♪

11:26.863 --> 11:28.697
♪ Where I do my mirror dance ♪

11:28.698 --> 11:32.067
♪ There's a hole in the wall
where the men can see it all ♪

11:32.068 --> 11:33.702
♪ But the women don't care ♪

11:33.703 --> 11:35.971
♪ 'Cause they're dancin'
in their underwear. ♪

11:35.972 --> 11:37.406
And their bras.

11:37.407 --> 11:39.308
Okay, that's great, Peter.
Time for your nap.

11:39.309 --> 11:43.913
But I'm not tired!
I want to go to the Clam and...

11:43.914 --> 11:45.047
Thank God.

11:45.048 --> 11:46.748
All right, just switch us all back.

11:48.819 --> 11:51.520
Oh, my God, the machine's been damaged.

11:51.521 --> 11:53.022
It's broken.

11:53.023 --> 11:55.658
Swapping four bodies
must've overloaded its system.

11:55.659 --> 11:57.760
If I can't fix it,
we'll be like this forever.

11:57.761 --> 11:59.495
This is a total disaster,

11:59.496 --> 12:01.597
like when the fat man
tried to keep Michael Shannon

12:01.598 --> 12:03.531
from going insane.

12:05.569 --> 12:08.537
Meadows. Snowflakes.

12:08.538 --> 12:10.739
Breeze through a cornfield.

12:10.740 --> 12:14.276
A soaring bird. Rain on a metal roof.

12:14.277 --> 12:16.745
Hey, Peter, I'm gonna need you
to fill out your time card.

12:16.746 --> 12:19.048
Yeah, sure, I'll have it
by the end of the day.

12:22.619 --> 12:24.286
It's okay.

12:24.287 --> 12:27.722
We'll hire new people
and start again tomorrow.

12:31.595 --> 12:33.863
We now return to a human hand

12:33.864 --> 12:35.130
rolling a ball of clay.

12:35.131 --> 12:36.432
- Yay!
- Yay!

12:36.433 --> 12:38.300
Stewie, enough of that.
We have to fix the machine.

12:40.737 --> 12:42.271
Oh, God, the fat man's friends.

12:42.272 --> 12:43.939
What's the black one's name?

12:43.940 --> 12:44.940
Cleveland.

12:44.941 --> 12:47.309
Cleveland, right. Cleveland!

12:47.310 --> 12:49.612
Peter, you name-remembering
son of a gun.

12:49.613 --> 12:51.280
Come on, we're heading down to the Clam.

12:51.281 --> 12:55.484
Oh, uh, wish I could, Glenn.
Sure could go for a pomtini.

12:55.485 --> 12:57.920
But, unfortunately,
I've got to help my son Chris

12:57.921 --> 12:59.088
with his homework.

12:59.089 --> 13:00.456
Well, if you finish early...

13:00.457 --> 13:02.591
They've all switched bodies. Let's go.

13:02.592 --> 13:03.792
That was close.

13:03.793 --> 13:05.227
All right, let's get upstairs and fix...

13:05.228 --> 13:07.463
Peter, come in
the kitchen, I need your help.

13:07.464 --> 13:09.965
What? Huh? I'm watching something.

13:09.966 --> 13:11.900
Oh, God, keep him busy.
I'll make this quick.

13:13.670 --> 13:16.071
Hey, I realized I can reach
the animal crackers,

13:16.072 --> 13:18.574
so I'm actually gonna be a while.

13:18.575 --> 13:20.509
Here, load these in the car.

13:20.510 --> 13:22.945
Are you going away for a long time?

13:22.946 --> 13:24.813
Oh, darn it. Darn.

13:24.814 --> 13:26.615
Please tell me you didn't forget.

13:26.616 --> 13:29.351
This is the weekend we're going
to Dr. Johann's retreat.

13:29.352 --> 13:32.922
Oh, uh, of course. Uh, remind me
what that is again.

13:32.923 --> 13:34.623
It's the couples' sex seminar

13:34.624 --> 13:37.493
where the husband learns
how to give his wife an orgasm.

13:37.494 --> 13:39.495
Wait, are you saying...

13:39.496 --> 13:41.664
we have to have sex this weekend?

13:41.665 --> 13:44.032
Yup. All weekend long.

13:45.835 --> 13:48.337
Now that's what I call a curveball.

13:50.307 --> 13:52.081
Commercial!

13:58.331 --> 14:00.365
Peter? Everything okay?

14:00.366 --> 14:02.567
You hardly spoke the whole car ride.

14:02.568 --> 14:05.070
Uh, I-I was simply thunderstruck

14:05.071 --> 14:08.572
at the prospect
of intercoursing you. Darling.

14:12.612 --> 14:14.079
Brian, you've got to help me.

14:14.080 --> 14:16.748
Lois signed us up for some
perverse, pee-pee-related class

14:16.749 --> 14:18.350
on the female orgasm. She's crazed.

